# Merging an exposure bracket, then developing it.
#
# Drop your bracket on one palette row -- hold ALT while dropping to make them
# a group in one go -- then right-click the group and "Sort by filename" so the
# order is what you expect.
#
# The chain reads in the order the work happens:
#
#   align       solves each frame against a common reference and attaches the
#               transform as a sidecar. It warps nothing itself; the merge
#               samples through it. A correction below half a pixel is skipped
#               when there is no other transform to fold into, because the
#               resample would cost more than the alignment gains.
#
#   merge_hdr   reads each frame's shutter, aperture and ISO from EXIF and
#               divides them out, so every frame contributes the same radiance
#               where it is well exposed. The result is scene-linear with real
#               headroom: a seven-frame bracket reaches 900-odd with the
#               midtones near 2.0. That is why it is displayed raw as well --
#               it is the data worth keeping, not yet a picture.
#
#   _develop.tgl  everything after the merge -- tone mapping, the develop
#               controls, denoise, and the creative effects -- lives in a
#               shared fragment that panorama.tgl includes too. The stage order
#               matters and is explained there; having one copy is what stops
#               the two scripts from drifting apart, which they already had.
#
# It reads and writes `developed`, so the merge assigns to that name and the
# display below reads it back.
#
# params() gives every stage its own group of sliders, so the whole pipeline
# is tunable from one panel.

# The tone mapper is a dropdown rather than a fixed stage, because which one to
# use is the comparison worth having in front of you:
#
#   tonemap        one curve for every pixel. No halos, trivially fast, and
#                  structurally unable to give the sky more of the display
#                  without taking the same from the land.
#   tonemap_local  splits illumination from detail and compresses only the
#                  illumination, recovering local contrast the global operator
#                  cannot. Watch strong edges for halos, which is its own
#                  failure mode.

tonemap_op = choose("tonemapper", [tonemap, tonemap_local])

developed = image("group")
         => params( align )()
         => params( merge_hdr )()
         => params( tonemap_op )()         

include "_develop.tgl"

display( developed, "final" )
